+### Modal execution
+
+`FrameRunner` runs synchronous script invocations on one same-thread stack,
+using `corosensei 0.3.4` through the platform execution seam. Its explicit
+8 MiB stack allocation is fallible. No VM pointer crosses threads.
+
+`Window.showModal()` shows and raises the window, suspends the caller and
+returns control to the outer event loop. Each subsequent frame advances
+engine time once, delivers modal input and timers on the suspended stack,
+and yields for presentation. Nested dialogs stay on that same stack and
+complete in reverse order. Every poll body has invocation-local state.
+The modal policy excludes background input and invalidates stale input
+batches when entering or leaving a dialog.
+
+Shutdown resumes the pending native call with a cancellation error and
+finishes every foreign frame normally before the coroutine is dropped;
+forced Rust unwinding across a native ABI frame is forbidden. The frame
+runner is destroyed before engine services in `GameHost`.
+
+Startup scripts still execute before the platform frame runner exists.
+Modal calls from that phase report a missing-driver error; they do not
+silently accept a choice. Self-updating transition completion callbacks
+still run inside the projection hook, outside the frame invocation; modal
+calls from those callbacks also return an explicit error. Supporting those
+entry points requires a frame-capable startup scheduler and a separate
+resumable post-projection phase. Neither limitation is bypassed with a
+fabricated result.
 
